00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:30.475
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:05:30.475 --> 00:05:32.380
So I'm not particularly
familiar with Python.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.630
It's not something I
for whatever reason just

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:35.870
happen to run into.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:37.320
So for those people who
aren't familiar with Python,

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.160
do you want to tell us a
little bit about the language,

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.620
and what it's good for, and
all that kind of fun stuff?

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.220
DUSTIN INGRAM: Python is
an interpretive language.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.790
So that means that it's not
compiled like C or something

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:46.300
like that.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.925
It's sort of every
time you run Python,

00:05:47.925 --> 00:05:49.330
it interprets the language.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:52.630
Python's also dynamically typed,
so unlike some other languages,

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.510
you don't have to
specify the types,

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:58.425
like if this is a string, or if
it's an integer, or whatever.

00:05:58.425 --> 00:06:00.550
And then more generally,
Python's just kind of like

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.500
a pretty high level language.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:03.605
It's super readable.

00:06:03.605 --> 00:06:05.980
So since the very beginning,
Python's had a really strong

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:07.580
focus on code readability.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.370
So a lot of people
like to say that Python

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:11.510
is like runnable pseudocode.

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:12.995
So it kind of looks
like something

00:06:12.995 --> 00:06:14.620
that you would write
that's pseudocode,

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.800
and it also kind of
looks just like Python.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.550
So for that reason,
people really like it.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.600
Beginners really like it because
it's easy to pick up and learn.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.820
It looks very readable.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.260
Folks just like
it when they want

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:24.887
to be able to read
their code, I guess.

00:06:24.887 --> 00:06:25.970
MARK MANDEL: So OK, great.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.590
So are there any particular
great use cases for Python?

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:31.090
Is there things you would be
like, this is the perfect thing

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.050
to use Python for?

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:33.633
DUSTIN INGRAM: I'm
totally biased here

00:06:33.633 --> 00:06:35.890
because I like to use
Python for everything.

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:38.140
And one of the really nice
things I think about Python

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:41.355
is that if you really want to,
you can use it for anything.

00:06:41.355 --> 00:06:43.480
Something else that people
like to say about Python

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.180
is that it's the second best
language for everything.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.082
So basically, if
you want to do it,

00:06:48.082 --> 00:06:49.540
you could probably
do it in Python.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:53.860
It might not be the fastest
or easiest 100% of the time,

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.550
but definitely possible to do
pretty much anything you want.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.020
Python is super popular
for web applications.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.600
So a lot of real big name
groups run their large services

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.100
on Python, so like Instagram,
for example, it's all Python.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.020
It's all Django.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.170
And other similar kind
of large scale things

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:13.190
like to use Python, and
data science as well.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:16.720
So a lot of folks do data
science and machine learning

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.120
and stuff with Python
mostly because that stuff

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.150
is hard enough as it is.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:22.000
So as long as you
can read your code

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.000
and write meaningful
code with Python,

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.060
it sort of helps abstract away
some of the more complex parts

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:28.950
of that, I guess.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.450
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, I do have to
have a follow up on this one,

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:32.170
though.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:33.898
You said almost everything.

00:07:33.898 --> 00:07:36.190
So what would you not use
Python for, or what would you

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:39.080
maybe not recommend people
use it as a first choice for?

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.038
DUSTIN INGRAM: So because
Python's interpreted,

00:07:41.038 --> 00:07:43.630
that means we're not compiling
something down to a binary.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:46.323
And it means that it's a
little bit slower to run.

00:07:46.323 --> 00:07:48.490
Just the fact that it's
interpreted makes it slower.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:49.532
So anything that's like--

00:07:49.532 --> 00:07:52.390
would be super performance
heavy, probably not

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:54.340
great to use with
Python, although there's

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.920
all sorts of workarounds for
making Python really fast.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:59.170
And I'm specifically
talking about the Python

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:03.070
interpreters is written in C.
So that's what we call C Python.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:08.440
And that sort of is a relatively
mediocre or slow interpreter.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.690
It's kind of designed to
work well and not necessarily

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.127
for performance optimizations.

00:08:12.127 --> 00:08:13.960
There are other Python
interpreters, though.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.420
So there's also PyPy, which
is self-hosted Python,

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:17.260
and it's real fast.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.510
So depending on
what you want to do,

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:21.920
you can kind of make
Python a little bit faster.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.730
But generally, if you're
doing anything that's really

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.663
CPU bound or IO bound,
Python's maybe not

00:08:27.663 --> 00:08:28.830
going to be the best choice.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:30.580
You might want to write
a little bit of C,

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.590
or a little bit of Rust,
or something like that.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:33.340
MARK MANDEL: I'm
sitting here trying

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:34.900
to think of terrible ideas.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.350
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh, we can have
a whole other conversation

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:37.700
about--

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:38.450
MARK MANDEL: That's
a whole episode.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:39.658
BRIAN DORSEY: Terrible ideas.

