So way back in the day, when App
Engine came out, which I think

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.540
was over 10 years ago now--

00:08:58.540 --> 00:08:59.290
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:01.570
DUSTIN INGRAM: It was one
of the very first platform

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.280
as a service type
things for Python.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.988
So it was kind of one
of the first places

00:09:04.988 --> 00:09:07.640
that you could take a Python
web application and deploy it.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:09.858
Obviously, it's changed
a lot since then.

00:09:09.858 --> 00:09:11.650
That's still something
that a lot of people

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:13.878
come to Google Cloud
to do with Python.

00:09:13.878 --> 00:09:14.920
We still have App Engine.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.090
We have Compute Engine as well.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.060
We have App Engine Flux
and Cloud Functions.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:21.393
And all these sort of play a
little bit different roles,

00:09:21.393 --> 00:09:23.530
but in the end, you're
kind of always deploying

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:24.990
a Python application or some--

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.250
for functions, it's just a
function to Google cloud.

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:28.000
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.520
How do you go
about like choosing

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.475
which platform is a good fit?

00:09:32.475 --> 00:09:34.975
Because you just said we've got
like seven different things.

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:38.520
It's fine.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.145
DUSTIN INGRAM: You
gotta roll the dice.

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:40.920
I don't know.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:41.620
Yeah, so we do.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:42.800
We have a lot.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:44.900
They do serve different
use cases, though.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:47.160
So let's start with the
original App Engine.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.480
The original App Engine made
a lot of choices for you

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:51.220
about what runtime
was available to you,

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:53.620
like system level
libraries, things like that.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.090
And you didn't get to
really change that too much.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.132
We sort of picked
a couple packages,

00:09:58.132 --> 00:10:00.340
put them in the runtime for
you, and they were there.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.470
You could bundle some extra
bytes on dependencies.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:03.553
It was pretty restrictive.

00:10:03.553 --> 00:10:05.290
And again, that
was 10 years ago,

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:06.850
so things have
changed a little bit.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:10.530
The new App Engine, you want
to use that if you kind of want

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.020
a consistent base image.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:13.510
So it's Ubuntu under the hood.

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:15.520
But you want to be able
to install like whatever

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:19.220
dependencies you want, anything
from PyPI or anywhere else.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:21.060
Yeah, and if you
just want to respond

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:24.060
to web traffic, that's what
App Engine is really good for.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.890
Cloud Functions, also
kind of like that.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:27.758
You can also sort
of write something

00:10:27.758 --> 00:10:30.300
that kind of looks like a web
application in Cloud Functions.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.300
But that would just be
basically a function that

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.680
responds to an HTTP request.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.200
So it would be like a single
end point in your application

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.550
would be a single function.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:40.900
But then if you don't
want to do HTTP at all,

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.920
Cloud Functions are great for
triggers across all of GCP.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:46.230
So one really common
example is you

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:48.750
want to do something with a
file after it's been uploaded

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:51.750
to your storage bucket, so Cloud
Functions is perfect for this

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.850
because you can sort of
hook into the events that

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.640
are happening, see every
time this file is out

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:57.890
of this bucket, do this thing.

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:00.057
And then you just write a
little function in Python.

00:11:00.057 --> 00:11:01.890
It gets some information
about the event,

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.060
and you can do whatever.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:04.977
And then beyond that,
the rest of the platform

00:11:04.977 --> 00:11:07.690
is starting to just add
additional customized ability.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:11.730
So like Flex, you can really
customize the runtime.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:15.323
If you need to do really
specific video encoding stuff--

00:11:15.323 --> 00:11:17.490
I know some of our customers
do that-- they use Flex

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:20.220
because they can just really
install exactly the system, all

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:22.710
the libraries, really
fast libraries, whatever

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.110
they need to do to do
a really specific task.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:26.340
It works well for that.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:28.560
MARK MANDEL: So here's
a fun question for you.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.330
I'm writing Python,
and I've decided

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.380
App Engine is the way I
want to go to begin with.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:33.690
And then I'm running
stuff on App Engine,

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.860
and I've realized maybe
I've made the wrong choice.

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:37.020
What does the
developer experience

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:38.437
look like for each
of those tools?

00:11:38.437 --> 00:11:40.020
Is it easy enough
for me to shift,

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:41.978
or I suppose it just
depends what I'm building,

00:11:41.978 --> 00:11:43.440
or what's your thoughts there?

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:43.980
DUSTIN INGRAM: It
kind of depends

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:44.980
on what you're building.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.700
And so yeah, let's just
talk about the latest--

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:51.040
the new App Engine
runtime is Python 3.7.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.910
We're calling it the idiomatic
App Engine standard runtime.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:56.250
Moving off of that would be
super easy because there's

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.160
nothing really locking you
into that runtime that's really

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:00.610
specific to App Engine, right?

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:03.640
Our getting started example,
for example, a lot of people

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:04.890
are super familiar with Flask.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:08.120
Flask is a micro framework for
web applications in Python.

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.870
And we just tell you
to write a Flask app,

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.640
and then you deploy
that to App Engine.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.320
And that Flask app, you
can take that anywhere.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:14.490
You can take that to Heroku.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:17.160
You can take that AWS,
anywhere you want.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:18.977
It's going to work
the exactly same way.

00:12:18.977 --> 00:12:21.060
There's not going to be
anything specific about it

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:21.750
for App Engine.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:22.890
So it's pretty portable.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:23.750
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, I
have a follow up here.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:26.430
You kind of alluded a couple
times to original App Engine,

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.710
or you kind of said things
are different a little now.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:31.080
So for Python people who've
been around for a while

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:34.650
and what they knew of App Engine
is when it first came out,

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:36.760
or they looked at it
six or eight years ago.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:37.500
What's changed?

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.700
What is new App Engine?

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:39.570
Why should I care?

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.580
MARK MANDEL: Are we in, like,
App Engine classic mode?

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.413
Is it like our original
recipe or something?

00:12:43.413 --> 00:12:44.940
[MUNCHING] Mm!

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:47.410
DUSTIN INGRAM: It's like a
retro App Engine and then

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:48.858
futuristic App Engine.

00:12:48.858 --> 00:12:50.400
I shouldn't call it
retro App Engine.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:51.660
So part of the problem
is they're both

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:53.040
called App Engine standard.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.620
One's at Python 2 runtime,
one's Python 3 runtime.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:58.980
So the big difference is well,
one's Python 2 and one's Python

00:12:58.980 --> 00:12:59.610
3.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.510
That's a huge win for
a lot of our users.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:05.130
So just having Python 3 runtime
that's immediately available

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:05.950
is really great.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:08.100
And then, yeah, Python
2 App Engine standard,

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.810
we make a lot of
decisions for you.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.810
We provide only a certain
set of system libraries

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:13.710
out of the box.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:15.750
It's harder and possible
to install other ones.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.458
If you want to install
your dependencies,

00:13:17.458 --> 00:13:19.665
like Python dependencies,
you kind of have to--

00:13:19.665 --> 00:13:21.790
what's called vendoring
them into your application.

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:24.230
So it's really like copying
the source code, including

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:25.500
at bundling it up
with your application

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.150
and deploying the
whole thing at once.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:29.430
The new App Engine
runtime is great

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:33.190
because you just specify a
file called requirements.txt.

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:35.880
This is a list of packages that
are available on the Python

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.110
Package Index.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.930
And then when your App
Engine instance gets created,

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:42.090
we go to PyPI, we
download the packages,

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.690
we install them
into the instance.

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.620
And then when your application
goes run, then it's there.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.750
So it removes a whole lot
of this developer pain

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.213
about rendering things.

00:13:50.213 --> 00:13:51.630
And it also makes
it easier for us

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:55.050
to install built distributions
and all that kind of stuff that

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.780
comes along with the sort of
idiomatic Python packaging

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:58.733
environment.

00:13:58.733 --> 00:14:00.150
MARK MANDEL: Here
at Google Cloud,

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:01.740
we definitely do a
lot of containers,

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:04.290
especially for workloads in
which may not necessarily

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:06.025
fit into that web paradigm.

00:14:06.025 --> 00:14:08.150
Are there any best practices
for running containers

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.233
with Python or anything
people should be aware of?

00:14:10.233 --> 00:14:12.930
DUSTIN INGRAM: Yeah, it's
pretty standard and idiomatic

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:13.660
container stuff.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:15.077
So if you're
familiar with Docker,

00:14:15.077 --> 00:14:17.090
I mean, using Python
with containers

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.080
is pretty straightforward.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:21.660
One thing about Python that
is kind of interesting-- it's

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.550
kind of hard to ship
a Python executable.

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:26.755
So something that would
not require an interpreter

00:14:26.755 --> 00:14:27.880
or something along with it.

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:30.578
So a lot times, people are
deploying Python in containers.

00:14:30.578 --> 00:14:32.370
They're installing
Python, and then they're

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:33.245
writing a Python app.

00:14:33.245 --> 00:14:35.278
And those two things
are kind of separated.

00:14:35.278 --> 00:14:37.320
There are a lot of kind
of third party approaches

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:41.010
to bundling together the
runtime and your user code

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:42.375
into a single thing.

00:14:42.375 --> 00:14:45.000
And then you can go install that
on a computer, user's machine.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:46.350
But if you're using containers--

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:47.740
Ubuntu ships with Python in it.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:48.657
So it's already there.

00:14:48.657 --> 00:14:51.032
You're just going to make sure
you have the right version

00:14:51.032 --> 00:14:52.510
and then go and write your code.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:53.218
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:14:53.218 --> 00:14:57.770
I'm convinced I want to
try some Python on GCP.

00:14:57.770 --> 00:14:59.550
And I'm a Python developer.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.675
Where do I get started?

00:15:01.675 --> 00:15:04.050
DUSTIN INGRAM: I really like
this about our documentation

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:04.590
right now.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:06.600
So this is kind of a secret.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:08.847
Cloud Functions under the
hood is just a Flask app.

00:15:08.847 --> 00:15:10.180
So we talked about Flask before.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.203
That's microservice
web framework.

00:15:12.203 --> 00:15:13.620
Under the hood,
that's just Flask.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:16.640
So for App Engine, when we
show in our documentation stuff

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.030
like that, we're just showing
you really simple examples

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:20.080
with Flask.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.038
So if you want to get
started, what I would say

00:15:22.038 --> 00:15:24.430
is go to the Flask
documentation,

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:27.550
and take their quick
start, and just try and get

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:30.560
a Flask little web application
running on your machine.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:32.590
And then see if you can
take that and bring it

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:34.240
to Google Cloud
because it should be

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.230
really straightforward, right?

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:36.730
It should just be
you take it, you

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.830
add a little bit of
a command line magic

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:40.780
to set up the project
and deploy it,

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.110
and then it should just work.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:43.693
So that's what I'd
recommend because I

00:15:43.693 --> 00:15:47.080
think that kind of shows the
nice path of taking an existing

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.650
application and bringing
that to Google Cloud.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:52.150
You don't need to write anything
special just for App Engine

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:53.770
or just for Cloud Functions.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:55.228
Then the other fun
thing you can do

00:15:55.228 --> 00:15:58.210
is you can take your individual
end points of your function

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.460
and then try and break that
apart into microservices

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:01.960
and put them on Cloud Functions.

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:04.085
Because then it's just sort
of just the same thing.

00:16:04.085 --> 00:16:05.585
MARK MANDEL: And
for someone like me

00:16:05.585 --> 00:16:07.300
who has never really
played with Python,

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:09.650
where should I go to
learn about Python itself?

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:11.025
DUSTIN INGRAM:
One thing I really

00:16:11.025 --> 00:16:13.390
love about Python, the
community and the documentation

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:16.270
is generally excellent,
very well maintained, just

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:17.673
super easy to follow usually.

00:16:17.673 --> 00:16:19.090
I'm definitely
biased because I've

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:21.560
been looking at the same
documentation for a long time.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:23.440
So I would recommend
docs.python.org

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:26.260
is all of the documentation
about specific Python

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:27.910
standard library and runtimes.

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:31.810
And then a huge part of Python
is the community of libraries

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:32.993
that come along with it.

00:16:32.993 --> 00:16:35.410
So when you install Python,
you get the Python interpreter

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:36.520
and your standard library.

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:39.460
And then if you want more, you
can come to the Python package

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:40.750
index and install--

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:42.250
last time I looked,
there was, like,

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:44.172
170,000 different projects.

00:16:44.172 --> 00:16:45.380
They all do different things.

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:48.590
So you can go and
install something there.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:50.980
All those things have their
own documentation generally.

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:51.730
It's pretty good.

00:16:51.730 --> 00:16:54.070
So I would start
with core Python.

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:55.570
If you take a Python
course, there's

00:16:55.570 --> 00:17:00.090
lots of community supported
Python 101 type things.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.960
BRIAN DORSEY: So as a
longtime Python user,

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:04.569
when you were kind
of exploring GCPs,

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:06.310
was there anything
that surprised or made

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:07.630
you really-- actually
surprised either way,

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:09.130
but kind of especially
like surprise

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:12.250
made you really happy about
how people do things on GCP

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:13.180
or how things worked?

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:14.722
DUSTIN INGRAM: Yeah,
so it's actually

00:17:14.722 --> 00:17:16.550
kind of funny because
my background,

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:19.400
I spent a lot of time working
on Python packaging stuff.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:22.250
So I'm the maintainer of
the Python Package Index.

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.047
I work on some of
the packaging tools

00:17:24.047 --> 00:17:25.880
for installing packages,
that kind of thing.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:29.440
And when I got hired to be a
developer advocate for Python,

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:30.460
in the back of my mind--

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:32.945
everyone has this
imposter syndrome thing.

00:17:32.945 --> 00:17:35.320
In the back of my mind, I was
like, do I really know that

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:35.860
much about--

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:38.930
I kind of know a lot about
this packaging thing.

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.800
I don't really know that much
about maybe Python in general.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.650
And then when I got hired, I
realized most people's problems

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:48.430
are with packaging stuff.

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:51.458
So I was pleasantly
surprised to find that.

00:17:51.458 --> 00:17:53.750
But that's not what you want
to hear, is that like, oh,

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:55.500
I was surprised that
our users were having

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:56.740
lots of packaging problems.

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:58.630
So I was software
consultant before.

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:01.360
I had not really
used GCP too much.

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:03.100
Used some of the other services.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:06.250
I remember when the original App
Engine came out and remembered

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:08.950
it being kind of weird
and all the things

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:10.085
that came along with it.

00:18:10.085 --> 00:18:11.710
And I didn't really
actually even know.

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:13.240
I guess, maybe we hadn't
even launched it yet,

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:14.710
so maybe that's
why I didn't know.

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:16.210
But I didn't know
that we were going

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:19.780
to get this cool new Python
3.7 App Engine runtime.

00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:21.730
And so when I found
out that we had that

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:24.320
or we were about to launch it,
I was like, this is so cool.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.320
This is exactly
what it should be.

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:28.580
I was really happy to see
that you can just install

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.173
dependencies, all that stuff.

00:18:30.173 --> 00:18:32.340
And that it solves a lot
of those packaging problems

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.690
that I had seen people
having, so I guess

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:36.940
it shouldn't have surprised
me that Google was working

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:38.560
on that before I started.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:40.780
But yeah, it was a
pleasant surprise for sure.

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:42.405
MARK MANDEL: On that
wavelength, what's

00:18:42.405 --> 00:18:43.847
been the most interesting or--

00:18:43.847 --> 00:18:45.430
yeah, let's just say
interesting thing

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:48.490
that you've seen somebody do
with Python that's kind of also

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:49.448
caught you by surprise?

00:18:49.448 --> 00:18:52.115
DUSTIN INGRAM: I remember we had
a Py Con a couple of years ago.

00:18:52.115 --> 00:18:53.580
I don't remember
which one it was.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:55.090
This was maybe like
five years ago,

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:58.940
so sort of before the time when
we had nice object recognition

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:01.630
machine learning algorithms
that you can just build a model

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:02.630
and plug it into Python.

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:04.588
I don't remember what
his name, but this fellow

00:19:04.588 --> 00:19:07.390
had-- basically, he was having
a problem with squirrels eating

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:10.060
the bird seed in his backyard.

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:11.620
So what he had done
with Python, he

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:15.070
had created this image
video recognition

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:17.470
thing that could pick out
a squirrel in an image.

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:20.620
And he had a water
gun on a [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:24.904
that could aim and squirt
some water at the squirrels.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.545
And I remember seeing
that at the time.

00:19:29.545 --> 00:19:31.920
I think it was either like a
talk or maybe a landing talk

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:32.825
at Python.

00:19:32.825 --> 00:19:34.200
And I remember
being like, wow, I

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.310
didn't know you could do
all that stuff with Python.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:37.180
Because that's so much.

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:38.347
It's controlling the motors.

00:19:38.347 --> 00:19:40.550
It's doing the image
recognition and all that stuff.

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:41.800
MARK MANDEL: That's fantastic.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:43.800
DUSTIN INGRAM: As far as
technically challenging

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.000
or interesting things people
have done with Python,

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.770
I mentioned before that there's
alternative interpreters

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:49.270
for Python.

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:53.035
So PyPy is the self-hosted
Python interpreter.

00:19:53.035 --> 00:19:54.910
I have dug into the
source code a little bit,

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:56.360
and it's mind boggling.

00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:59.170
But basically, it's
Python hosted on Python.

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:01.670
It's Python interpreting
other Python code.

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:03.850
So the point when a
language self-hosts itself

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:05.110
is pretty interesting.

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:08.350
And PyPy is not exactly Python.

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:10.840
But the point at which Python
started self-hosting itself,

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.580
I think, was a huge achievement.

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:14.200
Really technically
interesting to see

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:17.305
how you can write Python code
to interpret Python code.

00:20:17.305 --> 00:20:19.180
It goes right down the
rabbit hole basically.

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:22.010
But yeah, that's a pretty
interesting project as well.

00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:24.260
MARK MANDEL: Actually, you
said something interesting.

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.343
Before, you said there's
a C implementation that's

00:20:26.343 --> 00:20:28.910
not that fast and a Python
implementation that's

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:30.440
way faster?

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:33.960
DUSTIN INGRAM: Yeah, you might
be surprised to hear that.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.420
So I'm not a Python
core developer,

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:38.280
but I kind of like pay
attention to what's going on.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:41.000
I think the general feeling
about creating and working

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:43.250
on the Python interpreter
is that they're not really

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:45.520
trying to make it super
performance optimized.

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:47.120
They want it to be
bug free, and they

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:48.870
want to be able to add
new features to it,

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:51.500
have it be readable for
the core and developers.

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:54.410
And so, yeah, it doesn't
receive a whole lot

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:56.640
of optimization, which is fine.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:58.170
I think that's totally fine.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:00.185
PyPy is interesting
because-- and this

00:21:00.185 --> 00:21:02.810
is starting to get a little bit
outside of what I totally know.

00:21:02.810 --> 00:21:04.460
But they do a
whole bunch of just

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:07.708
in time, kind of like it kind
of looks like compiling Python

00:21:07.708 --> 00:21:08.500
beforehand, which--

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:08.960
MARK MANDEL: Oh, interesting.

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:11.420
DUSTIN INGRAM: Speeds it
up quite significantly.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.550
I've seen people say anywhere
from like 5 to 10 times faster

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:15.360
when using PyPy.

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:15.500
But--

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:15.890
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:17.480
DUSTIN INGRAM: It comes with
a whole bunch of caveats

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:18.467
as well, so beware.

