00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:20.810
My question is, Cloud
Functions seem cool.

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:23.270
We can connect all of the
pieces of the internet together.

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:24.687
I think of it as
kind of like glue

00:24:24.687 --> 00:24:26.570
for all of the events
and web requests

00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:27.530
that you might make
here and there.

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:29.120
But are they only good
for lightweight stuff,

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:31.520
or can I do things that are a
little more binary oriented,

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:32.540
like images and the like?

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:33.680
MARK MANDEL: It's a good
thing that you asked me

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:35.000
about images actually, Brian.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:36.360
Thank you so much.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.060
BRIAN DORSEY: Was
that leading enough?

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:40.518
MARK MANDEL: We'll have you
back on the podcast definitely.

00:24:40.518 --> 00:24:42.450
[LAUGHS] So this is cool.

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:43.680
I found out about this today.

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:45.513
And I was actually kind
of excited about it.

00:24:45.513 --> 00:24:48.530
So Cloud Functions
bundles ImageMagick

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:50.957
in with the runtime,
which is really handy.

00:24:50.957 --> 00:24:52.790
BRIAN DORSEY: Right,
that sounds super cool.

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:53.730
What's ImageMagick, though?

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:55.320
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
ImageMagick, yes.

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:57.407
So ImageMagick is
a little binary.

00:24:57.407 --> 00:24:59.240
You probably have it
on one of your distros,

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:00.750
if you're on Linux
or you run Mac,

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:03.740
or if you run Windows
subsystem for Linux on Windows.

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:05.840
Basically, it's a
command line utility

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.090
for doing just about
anything you could ever

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:11.150
think of ever doing to
images, from resizing,

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:13.280
cropping, blurring,
putting together

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:15.090
GIFs that are animated.

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:17.090
Visually, if you can
pretty much think about it,

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:18.690
you could probably do
it with ImageMagick.

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:19.760
BRIAN DORSEY: That
sounds like a challenge.

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:20.800
MARK MANDEL: Find me
something you can't do.

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:21.260
There we go.

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:21.968
That's the thing.

00:25:21.968 --> 00:25:25.750
Anyway, so apparently, it gets
bundled with Cloud Functions.

00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:27.400
Just so the binaries
there-- usually,

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:29.650
you can get a nice wrapper
library with your language.

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:31.210
I know JS has one, for example.

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:33.278
Or if you really want
to, just run convert.

00:25:33.278 --> 00:25:35.570
That's basically the command
line, and pass it through.

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:37.540
But you can do pretty
much whatever you want.

00:25:37.540 --> 00:25:39.707
So we're going to link to
a tutorial that's actually

00:25:39.707 --> 00:25:42.550
talking about joining Cloud
Functions with Vision API

00:25:42.550 --> 00:25:45.265
and ImageMagick to
blur offensive images.

00:25:45.265 --> 00:25:47.140
That will take you
through some of the steps.

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:48.160
BRIAN DORSEY: That
sounds useful.

00:25:48.160 --> 00:25:50.140
MARK MANDEL: But yeah,
essentially, ImageMagick

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:51.670
lives in the runtime.

00:25:51.670 --> 00:25:54.760
So either you can use a
wrapper or use it directly.

00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:56.510
So you can basically
do whatever you want.

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:59.140
So yeah, if you wanted to
build a dynamic image resizing

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:02.317
thing for a container or
bucket and then spit that out

00:26:02.317 --> 00:26:04.650
on the other side, you could
do that pretty easily, too.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.160
There's really kind of
no limit in some ways.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:07.190
BRIAN DORSEY: Wow,
that sounds awesome.

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:08.620
